No, the message is "_perhaps_ you'd better #quit" (my emphasis). If
the game really knew for sure that it wasn't worth continuing, it'd
panic instead; the impossible() message merely advises you that
Something Is Wrong and that you proceed at your own risk.

> A quick look at the source (function restmonchn in restore.c) hints that
> this message is apparently given when a saved game is restored, if some
> monster is wielding something that is not really classified as a weapon.
> Cockatrice corpses, for example, are some non-weapon items a monster may
> wield. 

Whether the object is a weapon or not isn't relevant; it's looking for
'obj->owornmask & W_WEP' (that is, the item is labelled as being
wielded), not 'obj->oclass == WEAPON_CLASS'. As Arthur observes, this
message is produced when none of the monster's items are so labelled,
even though the monster struct itself says that something should
be. NetHack's response to this is to say "ok, this monster isn't
wielding anything after all" and carry on, warning you just in case
this is a symptom of some larger problem.

